<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad Shah of Brunei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1402">1402</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Awang Alak Betatar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad Shah was, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a> tradition, the first Muslim sultan of Brunei. Born Awang Alak Betatar, he is said to have accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and taken the name Muhammad Shah in the 14th century, a conversion often dated to 1368. He founded the dynasty that still reigns in Brunei, with its early capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kota-batu">Kota Batu</a> on the Brunei River.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to tradition, Awang Alak Betatar and his half-brother Awang Semaun were among the 14 children of Dewa Emas Kayangan, born of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murut">Murut</a> woman from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limbang">Limbang</a>, and he was crowned though he was not the firstborn. His descent remains unclear, and it has been disputed whether his father was Dewa Emas Kayangan or Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-shah">Ibrahim Shah</a> of Kedah. His story is told chiefly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syair-awang-semaun">Syair Awang Semaun</a>, an oral poem first written down in 1947, and the Salasilah Raja-Raja Brunei, the royal genealogy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He first founded a village at Negeri Garang, in the present Temburong District, before moving to the waters around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kota-batu">Kota Batu</a>, where the river let him control the rural areas. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a> had been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire</a>; as Majapahit declined, his forces under Awang Jerambak defeated the Melanau leader Basiung at Igan, took Oya, Matu, Mukah and other Melanau lands and pressed on to Sambas, so that his frontier ran from the Tutong River to the Rejang delta.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syair-awang-semaun">Syair Awang Semaun</a> and the Salasilah say he embraced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> to marry Puteri Dayang Pingai, daughter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>; since Johor was not created until 1512, Jamil Al-Sufri reads it here as Tumasik, ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>. Islam did not yet reach deeply into a society shaped by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> ways. Envoys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Hongwu Emperor</a> reported in 1371 that he was cold towards them and spoke of an invasion by Sulu, but the mission brought letters acknowledging Chinese authority, carried by his envoy Ismail.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Later rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a> were styled sultans, and he is also regarded as the first sultan of Sarawak, Labuan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">Sabah</a>. Tradition says his daughter Princess Ratna Dewi married Ong Sum Ping, a Chinese immigrant who set up a trading station on the Kinabatangan River. The early succession is uncertain: Chinese records name rulers such as Raja Ma-na-je-chia-na whom the royal genealogy omits, naming Sultan Ahmad after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad Shah of Brunei” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The wars began under <b>Muhammad Shah</b> I (r. 1358–1375) and were marked, according to Ferishta's later chronicle, by great slaughter on both sides.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Brunei's own tradition, preserved in the royal genealogy and the epic Syair Awang Semaun, holds that its first ruler, <b>Awang Alak Betatar</b>, accepted Islam and became Sultan Muhammad Shah, an event traditionally placed in the 1360s; the register uses 1368.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
